Falsifies `inter_process_communication::verification::removing_a_waiter_preserves_the_invariant`.

The defect: `remove_sender` notes the hit and then keeps the victim anyway. The drain-and-repush
shape invites it: the loop's job reads as "rebuild the queue", the `else` is the only thing that
drops anything, and the function still returns the right answer to every caller that only asks
whether the waiter was there.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(!found || e.is_idle())`, on a hit. The invariant
assertion survives, because one queue is still empty, which is why this harness states both:
a remove that reports success while leaving the thread queued is how milestone 133 (ending a
permanently blocked thread) ends a thread and then finds it again on a wait queue.

diff --git a/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs b/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/inter_process_communication/src/lib.rs
@@ -271,6 +271,5 @@ impl<T: Node> Rendezvous<T> {
                 found = true;
-            } else {
-                // SAFETY: just popped from this queue, so it is valid and on no queue.
-                unsafe { kept.push_back(node) };
             }
+            // SAFETY: just popped from this queue, so it is valid and on no queue.
+            unsafe { kept.push_back(node) };
         }
